<% content_for :title, "Lexxy: collaborative rich text for Rails" %>
<% content_for :description, "lexxy-realtime makes Basecamp's Lexxy editor collaborative with a model macro, a form helper, and a generator. Several people can edit the same rich text field in Rails, and after each change the HTML is saved back to the record. Built on yrby." %>
<% content_for :canonical, canonical_url("/lexxy") %>

<section class="pt-6 pb-14 sm:pt-14">
  <h1 class="max-w-3xl font-serif text-5xl leading-[1.02] font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-6xl">
    Lexxy, collaborative.
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-zinc-400">
    Lexxy is Basecamp's rich text editor for Rails. It's built on Lexical
    and replaces Trix in Action Text forms. <code>lexxy-realtime</code>
    makes it collaborative with a model macro, a form helper, and a
    generator, so two people can type in the same field and watch each
    other's text and cursors as they go.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-4">
    <%= code_sheet "app/models/post.rb", "model" do %><div class="px-2 py-3"><%= sample_lexxy_model %></div><% end %>
    <%= code_sheet "app/views/posts/_form.html.erb", "view" do %><div class="px-2 py-3"><%= sample_lexxy_form %></div><% end %>
    <%= code_sheet "terminal", nil do %><div class="px-2 py-3"><%= sample_lexxy_install %></div><% end %>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Import <code>lexxy-realtime</code> next to Lexxy in your JavaScript.
    The generated channel denies every subscriber until you implement its
    <code>authorized?</code> method. After that, open the form in two
    browsers and type.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-4xl">
    <code>post.body</code> is still Action Text.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    While people edit, the document is CRDT state saved in yrby's
    <code>Y::Document</code> tables. After each recorded change,
    lexxy-realtime renders that state to HTML in Ruby with
    <code>Y::Lexxy</code> and saves it to <code>post.body</code>. The HTML
    matches what Lexxy itself would have submitted, byte for byte, so your
    search indexing, mailers, and show pages keep working with no changes.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    <%= link_to "Watch the saved HTML update as you type →", demo_path("lexxy"), rel: "nofollow",
                class: "text-accent underline decoration-rose-400/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-rose-400" %>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-4xl">
    Each field gets its own signed grant.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    The form helper renders a signed GlobalID for that record and attribute.
    The channel finds the record from it and then calls your
    <code>authorized?</code> method. A token signed for <code>:body</code>
    won't open any other field, and the channel rejects a tampered or expired
    one. The browser can only join the document your page rendered a token
    for.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-4xl">
    Custom nodes and encryption.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Custom Lexical nodes you've registered sync along with the rest of the
    document. If <code>Y::Lexxy</code> has no rule for a node, it still
    renders the node's text into the saved HTML.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    For encrypted fields, pass <code>encrypted: true</code> to
    <code>has_collaborative_rich_text</code>. The Action Text body and the
    CRDT state are both stored with Active Record encryption.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-4xl">
    Built on yrby.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    lexxy-realtime is a small layer over yrby, the library the rest of this
    site documents. It syncs with the y-websocket protocol over Action Cable
    or AnyCable, records each change before acknowledging it, and renders
    HTML in Ruby. With import maps, the generator pins JavaScript that ships
    in the gem. With a bundler, you install the <code>lexxy-realtime</code>
    npm package. Either way, the collaboration code uses the copy of Lexical
    that Lexxy already loads, so the page doesn't load a second one.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-5 space-y-1 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-500">
    <p><span class="text-accent">bundle add lexxy-realtime</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class="text-accent">bin/rails generate lexxy_realtime:install</span></p>
  </div>
  <nav class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-400">
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://github.com/jpcamara/lexxy-realtime">GitHub</a>
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://rubygems.org/gems/lexxy-realtime">RubyGems</a>
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/lexxy-realtime">npm</a>
    <%= link_to "Live demo", demo_path("lexxy"), rel: "nofollow", class: "hover:text-accent" %>
    <%= link_to "yrby docs", doc_path("getting-started"), class: "hover:text-accent" %>
  </nav>
</section>
